// A statement that compares instead of assigning.
//
// Assignment exists only as a statement, and only where the parser can see a
// statement-shaped one: `x = v`, or `xs[i] = v` with a plain identifier at the
// front. Anything else is an *expression* statement, and a bare `=` inside an
// expression is equality. So `(import xs)[i] = v;` compiles to a comparison
// whose result is discarded -- it type-checks, it links, it runs, and it does
// nothing at all.
//
// This is the language's worst trap, and the reason the `lset` idiom exists.
// Rejecting it costs nothing: a comparison evaluated for no reason is never
// what anybody meant.

chk_exprstmt(int id) {
  int i1_of_v = i1_of(id);
  string op = s1_of(i1_of_v);
  if(k_of(i1_of_v) == "bin" && is_asgop(op) == 1) {
    int i1_of_v2 = i1_of(id);
    string disc_hint_v = disc_hint(i1_of_v2);
    tc_err(id, "this statement compares instead of assigning: '=' in an expression is equality, so the statement has no effect" + disc_hint_v);
  }
} return void;

// Both spellings. idc.py's parser folds `=` into `==` on the way in, so by the
// time either compiler has an AST there is one operator; catching both here
// keeps the two agreeing about the same programs. It is the right net anyway --
// `x == 2;` as a statement is exactly as pointless as the assignment it is
// usually a typo for. `!=` is left out: nobody mistypes it for an assignment.
is_asgop(string op) {
  int ok = 0;
  if(op == "=" || op == "==") {
    ok = 1;
  }
} return int ok;

// Naming the fix is the point of the diagnostic, so an index target -- the
// case that actually bites -- gets told what to write instead.
disc_hint(int id) {
  string s = "";
  int i1_of_v = i1_of(id);
  if(k_of(i1_of_v) == "index") {
    s = " -- an imported list or a call result cannot be index-assigned directly; pass the list to a helper that takes it as a parameter, as lset(int[] xs, int i, int v) { xs[i] = v; }";
  }
} return string s;
